# =============================================================================
# Line-ending policy — this file exists to keep `sha256sum -c` meaningful
# =============================================================================
#
# The project's integrity model rests on one command returning 19/19:
#
#     cd docs/specs/v1.0 && sha256sum -c SPEC_MANIFEST_SHA256.txt
#
# That command hashes the WORKING COPY. On Windows, core.autocrlf=true rewrites
# LF to CRLF on checkout, which changes every byte count and therefore every
# hash — while the committed blob is untouched and perfectly correct.
#
# Discovered 2026-09-12: after a branch switch, a local run reported
# 00_PROJECT_MASTER_CONTEXT.md as FAILED. The blob was LF, 13,882 bytes,
# sha256 005d0ef9… — an exact match for the manifest. The working copy was
# CRLF, 14,145 bytes. Nothing was damaged; the CHECK was lying.
#
# That is the dangerous failure mode, and not because of the false alarm. The
# same mechanism can produce a false PASS: a file that git has not
# re-materialised since it was written still has its original endings, so the
# check passes for a reason unrelated to the file being correct. A guard that
# can be right by accident is not a guard.
#
# CI on ubuntu-latest checks out LF and was never affected — which is exactly
# why the discrepancy could have sat here unnoticed.

# --- FROZEN SPECIFICATION — never convert, ever -----------------------------
# -text disables all line-ending conversion, so the working copy stays
# byte-identical to the blob on every platform and the checksum check means
# what it claims to mean.
docs/specs/v1.0/**          -text

# --- MANIFESTS AND CHECKSUMMED ARTIFACTS ------------------------------------
# Fixtures carry per-slice checksums; evidence files are hashed and cited.
# Both must round-trip byte-for-byte.
*.sha256                    -text
SPEC_MANIFEST_SHA256.txt    -text
spikes/**/fixtures/**       -text
spikes/**/fixtures_proposal/** -text
data/manifests/**           -text

# --- EVERYTHING ELSE: normalise to LF in the repository ---------------------
# Stored as LF, checked out as LF. One representation, no surprises, and diffs
# stay readable across the team's machines.
*.py      text eol=lf
*.js      text eol=lf
*.json    text eol=lf
*.yaml    text eol=lf
*.yml     text eol=lf
*.md      text eol=lf
*.txt     text eol=lf
*.html    text eol=lf
*.sh      text eol=lf

# --- BINARY -----------------------------------------------------------------
*.png     binary
*.jpg     binary
*.apk     binary
*.zip     binary
*.nrrd    binary
*.npy     binary
